<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamenz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamenz is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, near the Sorbian-speaking area. In 1346 it was one of the founding members of the Six Towns League of Upper Lusatia, formed to protect trade. The writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</a> was born there in 1729.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamenz.jpg" alt="Kamenz" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dabbelju · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamenz_city_centre_aerial_view_north.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kamenz, in Sorbian Kamjenc, lies in the west of the historic region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, about 40 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and 30 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a>, on the Black Elster river between the West Lusatian Hills and the Lusatian Highlands. The town was built on greywacke and granite rocks that were mined there for centuries. On the Hutberg hill west of the centre a landscape park was laid out in 1893, known for its rhododendron and azalea collections, and the Lessing Tower was put up on the hilltop in 1864.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement arose in the late 12th century around a fortress built to secure the junction of the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-regia">Via Regia</a> trade route with the Black Elster. The route linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> with Silesia. Records first mention the town in 1225, and it became an independent city in 1319, when Emperor Louis IV enfeoffed the West Lusatian lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. In 1346 its citizens joined the Lusatian League for protection against robber barons and to keep the peace.</p><p>Hussite forces besieged and occupied it in 1429. In 1469 it passed with the League to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and in 1490 it returned to the Bohemian crown, under Vladislaus II, who had a Franciscan monastery founded north of the walls in 1493.</p><p>The citizens turned Protestant in 1536. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> of 1546–47 the Upper Lusatian towns refused to support Ferdinand I against the Protestant princes and lost many privileges. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1635">Peace of Prague</a> of 1635 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatias</a> passed to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Fires destroyed large parts of the old town in 1707 and again on 4 and 5 August 1842. From 1944 to 1945 a subcamp of the Gross-Rosen concentration camp stood in the town, where forced labourers worked for an aircraft-engine factory. After the war Kamenz was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kamenz is a town in the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and is known as the birthplace of the philosopher and poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</a> (1729–1781); a Lessing Museum opened in 1931. Its sights include the Late Gothic St Mary's church, built from 1275 to 1479 and said to be the only church built from granite north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and the St Anne abbey church, finished in 1510, which holds five carved wooden altarpieces of the 16th century. The Andreas Fountain on the marketplace dates from 1570.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kamenz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1346 Bautzen, Görlitz, Zittau, <b>Kamenz</b>, Löbau and Lauban, towns of the Margraviate of Upper Lusatia, then a land of the Kingdom of Bohemia, formed a league to suppress robber knights and protect their trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusatian-league">Lusatian League</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1346 Bautzen, Görlitz, Zittau, Lauban, <b>Kamenz</b> and Löbau formed the Six Towns League, protecting trade routes.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-upper-lusatia">Margraviate of Upper Lusatia</a></li></ul></section>
